-- 长篇匹配 1: 幸福与金钱
('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> The relationship between money and happiness has fascinated philosophers, economists, and psychologists for centuries. Conventional wisdom once held that happiness increased with income indefinitely. However, a landmark 2010 study by Nobel laureate Daniel Kahneman and Angus Deaton challenged this assumption, finding that emotional well-being rises with income but only up to about $75,000 per year. Beyond this threshold, additional income had a negligible impact on day-to-day happiness.</p><p><b>[B]</b> More recent research has complicated this picture. A 2021 study by Matthew Killingsworth, using real-time mood tracking via smartphone apps, found that happiness continues to rise with income well beyond the $75,000 threshold. Killingsworth suggested that the earlier plateau effect might have been an artifact of the measurement methods used rather than a genuine psychological ceiling. The debate highlights the methodological challenges inherent in studying subjective well-being.</p><p><b>[C]</b> Regardless of where the income-happiness curve flattens, researchers agree that how people spend their money matters at least as much as how much they earn. Spending on experiences—travel, concerts, dining with friends—tends to produce more lasting happiness than spending on material possessions. Experiences create memories and strengthen social bonds, whereas the novelty of material purchases typically fades quickly, a phenomenon psychologists call hedonic adaptation.</p><p><b>[D]</b> The social context of spending also plays a crucial role. Prosocial spending—using money to benefit others through charitable donations or gifts—has been consistently linked to greater happiness than spending on oneself. This finding holds across cultures and income levels, suggesting a fundamental psychological mechanism at work. Neuroimaging studies have shown that charitable giving activates the same reward centers in the brain as receiving money.</p><p><b>[E]</b> Time affluence—the subjective sense of having sufficient free time—emerges as another important factor. People who prioritize time over money tend to report higher levels of happiness. This is particularly relevant in modern societies where the constant pressure to maximize productivity can lead to "time poverty," a state of chronic busyness that undermines well-being despite material comfort.</p><p><b>[F]</b> Financial security, distinct from absolute wealth, appears to be especially important for psychological well-being. Having an adequate emergency fund, manageable debt levels, and stable income provides a psychological safety net that reduces anxiety and allows people to focus on meaningful pursuits. This explains why sudden wealth losses tend to have a more dramatic negative impact on happiness than gradual income gains have a positive one—a phenomenon known as loss aversion.</p><p><b>[G]</b> Commuting emerges in multiple studies as one of the most reliably negative experiences in daily life. Long commutes, particularly by car in heavy traffic, impose significant psychological costs that even higher salaries often fail to compensate for. This finding has important implications for urban planning and workplace policies regarding remote work.</p><p><b>[H]</b> Cross-cultural research reveals interesting variations in the money-happiness relationship. In more collectivist societies, social relationships and community ties often outweigh financial considerations as predictors of well-being. In more individualistic cultures, personal achievement and financial success tend to carry greater psychological weight. These differences underscore the importance of cultural context in understanding human happiness.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"Research using smartphone mood tracking found that happiness may continue increasing with income beyond previously identified limits."},{"label":"2","text":"The negative psychological impact of losing money is stronger than the positive effect of gaining the same amount."},{"label":"3","text":"The importance of financial factors versus social relationships for well-being varies between individualistic and collectivist cultures."},{"label":"4","text":"A 2010 study suggested that beyond approximately $75,000 per year, additional income has little effect on emotional well-being."},{"label":"5","text":"Long daily travel to work typically reduces happiness more than higher pay can compensate for."},{"label":"6","text":"Giving money to others produces happiness by activating the same brain regions that are stimulated by receiving money."},{"label":"7","text":"Feeling that one has enough free time contributes significantly to happiness."},{"label":"8","text":"The pleasure derived from buying physical objects diminishes faster than happiness from experiences."},{"label":"9","text":"Having adequate savings and stable finances provides peace of mind that supports overall well-being."},{"label":"10","text":"The debate about the income-happiness relationship illustrates the difficulty of measuring subjective experiences scientifically."}]',
 'B,F,H,A,G,D,E,C,F,B',
 '1→B(Killingsworth手机app研究) 2→F(loss aversion损失厌恶) 3→H(individualistic vs collectivist) 4→A(Kahneman $75,000研究) 5→G(commuting通勤) 6→D(慈善捐款激活奖励中枢) 7→E(time affluence时间充裕) 8→C(hedonic adaptation享乐适应) 9→F(financial security财务安全) 10→B(方法论争议)',
 7, '长篇匹配,幸福,金钱,心理学'),

-- 长篇匹配 2: 睡眠科学
('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> Sleep, once dismissed as a passive state of rest, is now recognized as a complex biological process essential for physical health, cognitive function, and emotional regulation. During sleep, the brain performs critical maintenance tasks, including consolidating memories, clearing metabolic waste products, and regulating hormone levels. The glymphatic system, discovered in 2012, flushes toxins from the brain during deep sleep, potentially explaining the link between poor sleep and neurodegenerative diseases like Alzheimer.</p><p><b>[B]</b> The modern epidemic of sleep deprivation has been described by the World Health Organization as a public health crisis. An estimated two-thirds of adults in developed nations fail to obtain the recommended eight hours of sleep per night. The causes are multifaceted: the proliferation of screens emitting sleep-disrupting blue light, the blurring of work-life boundaries in the digital economy, and the cultural valorization of busyness and productivity at the expense of rest.</p><p><b>[C]</b> The consequences of chronic sleep deprivation extend far beyond mere fatigue. Research has linked insufficient sleep to increased risks of obesity, diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and impaired immune function. Cognitive effects include reduced attention, impaired decision-making, and diminished creativity. Perhaps most alarmingly, drowsy driving is estimated to cause more accidents than alcohol-impaired driving in some jurisdictions.</p><p><b>[D]</b> The relationship between sleep and mental health is bidirectional. While psychiatric conditions often disrupt sleep patterns, chronic sleep problems can also trigger or exacerbate mental health issues. Treating sleep disorders has been shown to improve outcomes for conditions ranging from depression to anxiety. This has led some researchers to argue that sleep should be a primary target in mental health interventions.</p><p><b>[E]</b> Technological interventions for sleep improvement have proliferated in recent years. Sleep tracking devices, from wearable sensors to smart mattresses, promise to monitor sleep quality and provide actionable insights. However, some sleep specialists have expressed concern about "orthosomnia"—an unhealthy obsession with achieving perfect sleep scores that can paradoxically worsen sleep quality by increasing anxiety.</p><p><b>[F]</b> Chronobiology research has revealed significant individual differences in sleep timing preferences, known as chronotypes. "Morning larks" naturally wake early and are most productive in the morning, while "night owls" prefer later bedtimes and reach peak alertness in the evening. These differences are influenced by genetics and age, with adolescents typically experiencing a shift toward later chronotypes—a finding that has prompted calls for later school start times.</p><p><b>[G]</b> The economic costs of sleep deprivation are staggering. A landmark study by RAND Corporation estimated that insufficient sleep costs the US economy up to $411 billion annually in lost productivity. Similar studies in other developed nations have found comparable economic impacts. These figures have prompted some corporations to implement sleep-friendly policies, including nap rooms and flexible working hours.</p><p><b>[H]</b> Sleep hygiene—the set of practices that promote healthy sleep—has become a focus of public health campaigns. Recommendations include maintaining consistent sleep-wake schedules, avoiding screens before bedtime, limiting caffeine and alcohol consumption, and creating dark and cool sleeping environments. While these practices are well-supported by evidence, their implementation often requires broader lifestyle changes that many find challenging to sustain.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"Workplace policies like nap rooms and flexible hours are being adopted as companies recognize the high cost of sleep-deprived employees."},{"label":"2","text":"Individual genetic differences determine whether someone naturally prefers morning or evening activity patterns."},{"label":"3","text":"Excessive concern about sleep tracking data can create anxiety that makes sleeping more difficult."},{"label":"4","text":"During deep sleep, the brain removes harmful substances through a recently discovered cleaning mechanism."},{"label":"5","text":"Insufficient sleep is associated with serious physical conditions including heart problems and weakened immunity."},{"label":"6","text":"Treating sleep problems may help alleviate various mental health conditions."},{"label":"7","text":"The blue light from electronic devices is one factor contributing to widespread sleep insufficiency."},{"label":"8","text":"The financial impact of sleep deprivation on national economies amounts to hundreds of billions of dollars."},{"label":"9","text":"Public health advice focuses on practices like regular sleep schedules and avoiding stimulants."},{"label":"10","text":"Fatigued driving may cause as many or more traffic accidents than drunk driving in some areas."}]',
 'G,F,E,A,C,D,B,G,H,C',
 '1→G(企业nap rooms) 2→F(chronotypes基因决定) 3→E(orthosomnia) 4→A(glymphatic system) 5→C(肥胖糖尿病心血管) 6→D(bidirectional睡眠与心理健康) 7→B(蓝光屏幕) 8→G(RAND $411B) 9→H(sleep hygiene) 10→C(drowsy driving)',
 7, '长篇匹配,睡眠,健康,科技'),

-- 长篇匹配 3: 食物浪费
('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> Approximately one-third of all food produced globally—roughly 1.3 billion tons annually—is lost or wasted, according to the UN Food and Agriculture Organization. This staggering figure represents not only a moral failure in a world where nearly 700 million people go hungry but also an environmental catastrophe, as food waste generates an estimated 8% of global greenhouse gas emissions.</p><p><b>[B]</b> The distinction between food loss and food waste is important. Food loss occurs primarily at the production and distribution stages, particularly in developing countries where inadequate infrastructure for storage, transportation, and refrigeration leads to spoilage. Food waste, by contrast, occurs mainly at the retail and consumer levels, especially in developed nations where food is relatively inexpensive and cosmetic standards lead to the discarding of perfectly edible produce.</p><p><b>[C]</b> In developed countries, consumer behavior is a major driver of food waste. Studies show that households discard food for various reasons: overpurchasing due to bulk discounts, confusion about date labels ("sell by," "use by," "best before"), and a cultural aversion to eating leftovers. The average American family of four throws away approximately $1,500 worth of food each year.</p><p><b>[D]</b> Retail practices also contribute significantly to the problem. Supermarkets often reject produce that fails to meet strict cosmetic standards for size, shape, and color, even when the food is perfectly nutritious. "Ugly" fruits and vegetables are left to rot in fields or disposed of before reaching store shelves. Some retailers have begun selling imperfect produce at discounted prices, but such initiatives remain limited in scale.</p><p><b>[E]</b> France has emerged as a global leader in food waste reduction through legislative action. In 2016, the country passed a law requiring supermarkets to donate unsold food that is still safe for consumption to charities rather than destroying it. The law also established a hierarchy of actions, prioritizing prevention, followed by donation, animal feed, composting, and only as a last resort, landfill disposal.</p><p><b>[F]</b> Technological solutions are increasingly being deployed to address food waste. Smart refrigerators can track expiration dates and suggest recipes using items nearing spoilage. Mobile apps connect consumers with restaurants and grocery stores offering surplus food at reduced prices. Artificial intelligence systems help retailers optimize inventory management to reduce overstocking.</p><p><b>[G]</b> The environmental case for reducing food waste is compelling. When food decomposes in landfills, it releases methane, a greenhouse gas far more potent than carbon dioxide. Moreover, the resources used to produce wasted food—water, land, energy, labor—are also squandered. The water footprint of global food waste is estimated to be three times the volume of Lake Geneva.</p><p><b>[H]</b> Individual actions, while seemingly small, can collectively make a significant difference. Planning meals, storing food properly, understanding date labels, embracing imperfect produce, and composting food scraps are all effective strategies. Schools and community organizations are increasingly incorporating food waste education into their programs, recognizing that behavior change at the household level is essential to addressing this global challenge.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"Smart applications and AI are being developed to help both consumers and businesses reduce the amount of food thrown away."},{"label":"2","text":"One country has legally required supermarkets to give unsold edible food to charitable organizations."},{"label":"3","text":"The difference between food lost during production and food discarded by consumers is an important conceptual distinction."},{"label":"4","text":"Strict appearance requirements for fruits and vegetables result in nutritious food being discarded before reaching consumers."},{"label":"5","text":"Rotting food in landfills produces a greenhouse gas significantly more powerful than carbon dioxide."},{"label":"6","text":"A typical American household loses over a thousand dollars annually through discarded food."},{"label":"7","text":"The total amount of food wasted globally reaches more than one billion tons each year."},{"label":"8","text":"Simple personal strategies like meal planning and understanding food labels can help tackle the waste problem."},{"label":"9","text":"Ambiguous date marking on food products contributes to unnecessary household disposal."},{"label":"10","text":"The water used to produce food that is never eaten represents an enormous additional environmental cost."}]',
 'F,E,B,D,G,C,A,H,C,G',
 '1→F(AI和app) 2→E(France law) 3→B(food loss vs waste) 4→D(cosmetic standards) 5→G(methane) 6→C($1500/year) 7→A(1.3 billion tons) 8→H(individual actions) 9→C(date labels) 10→G(water footprint)',
 7, '长篇匹配,食物浪费,环保'),

-- 长篇匹配 4: 太空探索
('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> The modern space race, increasingly driven by private enterprise rather than government agencies, has entered a new era of commercial viability and technological ambition. Companies like SpaceX, Blue Origin, and Virgin Galactic have demonstrated that private capital and entrepreneurial innovation can achieve what was once the exclusive domain of superpower nations with vast public budgets.</p><p><b>[B]</b> The economics of space exploration have been transformed by reusable rocket technology. Traditional rockets, discarded after a single use, made space access prohibitively expensive. SpaceX Falcon 9 first-stage boosters, capable of landing vertically on drone ships and being reused multiple times, have reduced launch costs by roughly 60%. This cost reduction has opened new possibilities for satellite deployment, space station resupply, and even space tourism.</p><p><b>[C]</b> Satellite technology has become deeply integrated into modern life, supporting everything from GPS navigation and weather forecasting to telecommunications and financial transactions. The miniaturization of satellites, particularly the development of CubeSats—small, standardized spacecraft weighing just a few kilograms—has dramatically lowered the barrier to entry for space-based services. Universities and even high schools can now build and launch their own satellites.</p><p><b>[D]</b> Space debris presents a growing threat to orbital infrastructure. An estimated 34,000 objects larger than 10 centimeters are currently tracked in Earth orbit, along with millions of smaller fragments. At orbital velocities, even a fleck of paint can cause significant damage. The 2009 collision between an active Iridium communications satellite and a defunct Russian satellite created thousands of new debris fragments, illustrating the cascading risks of orbital congestion.</p><p><b>[E]</b> The scientific case for human space exploration extends beyond the practical benefits of satellite technology. Astronauts on the International Space Station conduct experiments in microgravity that cannot be replicated on Earth, advancing our understanding of fundamental physics, biology, and materials science. Research on the ISS has contributed to advances in drug development, water purification, and our understanding of bone density loss, which has applications for treating osteoporosis.</p><p><b>[F]</b> Mars colonization has emerged as the most ambitious goal of the new space age. SpaceX Elon Musk has articulated a vision of establishing a self-sustaining city on Mars, arguing that making humanity a multi-planetary species is essential for long-term survival. Critics, however, point to the enormous technical challenges: radiation exposure during the journey, the harsh Martian environment, and the psychological toll of prolonged isolation.</p><p><b>[G]</b> The militarization of space raises complex geopolitical concerns. While the 1967 Outer Space Treaty prohibits the placement of weapons of mass destruction in orbit, subsequent agreements have been less comprehensive. Anti-satellite weapons tested by several nations have demonstrated the vulnerability of orbital assets. The establishment of the US Space Force in 2019 signaled a recognition of space as a potential domain of conflict.</p><p><b>[H]</b> The question of who benefits from space exploration has become increasingly prominent. Critics argue that the resources devoted to space could be better spent addressing pressing earthly problems like poverty, disease, and climate change. Proponents counter that space technology has already delivered enormous benefits—from weather satellites that save lives through early warning systems to communications satellites that connect remote communities—and that continued investment will yield further dividends for humanity.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"Very small standardized spacecraft have made it possible for educational institutions to participate in space exploration."},{"label":"2","text":"A 1967 international agreement bans weapons of mass destruction in space but does not cover all military activities."},{"label":"3","text":"The ability to recover and reuse rocket components has substantially reduced the cost of reaching space."},{"label":"4","text":"Debate exists over whether space exploration funding would be better directed toward solving problems on Earth."},{"label":"5","text":"Experiments conducted in weightless conditions have practical medical applications for Earth-based patients."},{"label":"6","text":"The crash between two satellites demonstrated how one collision can generate thousands of additional hazards."},{"label":"7","text":"Establishing a human settlement on another planet is proposed as insurance for the survival of our species."},{"label":"8","text":"Modern space ventures increasingly involve private companies rather than government space programs."},{"label":"9","text":"Testing of weapons that can destroy satellites has shown how fragile our orbital infrastructure is."},{"label":"10","text":"GPS, financial systems, and weather predictions all depend on technology in Earth orbit."}]',
 'C,G,B,H,E,D,F,A,G,C',
 '1→C(CubeSats) 2→G(Outer Space Treaty) 3→B(reusable rockets) 4→H(critics vs proponents) 5→E(ISS bone density) 6→D(Iridium collision) 7→F(Musk multi-planetary) 8→A(private enterprise) 9→G(anti-satellite weapons) 10→C(satellite applications)',
 7, '长篇匹配,太空,科技,经济'),

-- 长篇匹配 5: 文化全球化
('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> Globalization has created unprecedented cultural exchange, enabling ideas, art forms, and traditions to cross borders with ease. The flow of cultural products—films, music, literature, cuisine—has enriched societies worldwide, exposing people to diverse perspectives and fostering cross-cultural understanding. Yet this very interconnectedness has also raised concerns about cultural homogenization and the erosion of local traditions.</p><p><b>[B]</b> The dominance of Hollywood films and American popular music in global markets exemplifies the asymmetrical nature of cultural globalization. In many countries, domestic film industries struggle to compete with the production values and marketing budgets of major American studios. This has led some nations to implement cultural protection policies, such as screen quotas requiring a minimum percentage of domestic content in cinemas.</p><p><b>[C]</b> The culinary landscape has been profoundly reshaped by globalization. Italian pasta, Japanese sushi, Indian curry, and Mexican tacos have become global staples, adapted to local tastes in ways that differ significantly from their original forms. This process of culinary hybridization illustrates both the benefits and complexities of cultural exchange: while food diversity has expanded dramatically, concerns about authenticity and cultural appropriation persist.</p><p><b>[D]</b> Language represents one of the most sensitive dimensions of cultural globalization. English has become the de facto global lingua franca, essential for international business, science, and diplomacy. While this facilitates global communication, it also threatens linguistic diversity. UNESCO estimates that one language dies approximately every two weeks, and that half of the world approximately 7,000 languages may disappear by the end of this century.</p><p><b>[E]</b> Social media platforms have become powerful vehicles for cultural transmission, enabling trends to go viral across continents within hours. K-pop phenomenon BTS achieving global popularity, Nigerian Afrobeats influencing Western pop music, and Spanish-language reggaeton dominating streaming charts all demonstrate that cultural influence flows in multiple directions, not simply from West to East or North to South.</p><p><b>[F]</b> The concept of cultural appropriation has generated intense debate. When elements of a marginalized culture are adopted by members of a dominant culture, questions arise about power dynamics, respect, and economic benefit. Critics argue that appropriation often strips cultural elements of their original meaning and context, while defenders emphasize that cultural borrowing has been a universal feature of human history and can be done respectfully.</p><p><b>[G]</b> Tourism, while economically beneficial, can have complex effects on local cultures. The transformation of sacred sites into tourist attractions, the commodification of traditional crafts for the souvenir market, and the staging of "authentic" cultural performances for visitors all raise questions about the impact of the tourist gaze on living cultures. Some communities have successfully developed cultural tourism models that preserve and celebrate traditions while generating sustainable income.</p><p><b>[H]</b> Indigenous communities face particular challenges in navigating globalization. On one hand, global connectivity has provided new platforms for indigenous voices to advocate for their rights and share their cultures. On the other hand, the pressures of economic integration often undermine traditional livelihoods, and the allure of urban lifestyles draws young people away from their communities, threatening intergenerational cultural transmission.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"The disappearance of languages is happening at a rate of about one every two weeks according to the UN cultural organization."},{"label":"2","text":"Foods like pizza and curry have been transformed as they spread globally, raising questions about authenticity."},{"label":"3","text":"Indigenous peoples face a dual-edged situation: new platforms for advocacy but pressure on traditional ways of life."},{"label":"4","text":"Some countries have mandated that theaters show a minimum amount of locally produced movies."},{"label":"5","text":"Musical styles from Korea, Nigeria, and Latin America demonstrate that cultural influence does not only flow from dominant Western powers."},{"label":"6","text":"The debate about whether borrowing from other cultures is respectful or exploitative has no simple resolution."},{"label":"7","text":"The conversion of religious locations into sightseeing destinations can have problematic effects on local communities."},{"label":"8","text":"Globalization enriches societies by exposing them to different viewpoints and traditions."},{"label":"9","text":"English serves as the common language for international commerce and research worldwide."},{"label":"10","text":"Some traditional communities have found ways to benefit economically from tourism while maintaining their cultural practices."}]',
 'D,C,H,B,E,F,G,A,D,G',
 '1→D(UNESCO language death) 2→C(culinary hybridization) 3→H(indigenous communities) 4→B(screen quotas) 5→E(K-pop, Afrobeats) 6→F(cultural appropriation) 7→G(tourism sacred sites) 8→A(cultural exchange) 9→D(lingua franca) 10→G(sustainable cultural tourism)',
 7, '长篇匹配,文化全球化,语言,旅游'),

-- 长篇匹配 6: 可再生能源
('LONG_MATCH',
 '<p><b>[A]</b> The global transition from fossil fuels to renewable energy sources represents one of the most significant economic transformations since the Industrial Revolution. Driven by the urgent need to address climate change and by the rapidly falling costs of renewable technologies, this energy transition is reshaping industries, geopolitics, and everyday life.</p><p><b>[B]</b> Solar power has experienced the most dramatic cost reductions of any energy technology in history. The price of solar photovoltaic modules has fallen by approximately 90% since 2010, making solar power cost-competitive with fossil fuels in many regions without subsidies. This cost revolution has been driven by economies of scale in manufacturing, particularly in China, and by continuous technological improvements in panel efficiency.</p><p><b>[C]</b> Wind energy has followed a similar trajectory, with the cost of onshore wind power declining by about 70% over the past decade. Modern wind turbines are engineering marvels, with the largest models standing over 250 meters tall and generating enough electricity to power thousands of homes. Offshore wind, while more expensive, benefits from stronger and more consistent winds and is experiencing rapid growth, particularly in Northern Europe.</p><p><b>[D]</b> The intermittency of solar and wind power—the fact that the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow—remains the central technical challenge of the renewable energy transition. Energy storage solutions, particularly lithium-ion batteries, are advancing rapidly but remain expensive at the grid scale needed for deep decarbonization. Pumped hydro storage, which uses excess electricity to pump water uphill and then releases it through turbines when needed, remains the most widely deployed large-scale storage technology.</p><p><b>[E]</b> The geopolitics of energy are being fundamentally reshaped by the renewable transition. Countries that have historically depended on energy imports can achieve greater energy independence through domestic renewable generation. Conversely, nations whose economies depend on fossil fuel exports face existential challenges. The transition is also creating new dependencies, particularly on the supply chains for critical minerals like lithium, cobalt, and rare earth elements used in batteries and other clean energy technologies.</p><p><b>[F]</b> The concept of a "just transition" has gained prominence, recognizing that the shift away from fossil fuels must address the needs of workers and communities whose livelihoods depend on carbon-intensive industries. Coal mining regions, in particular, require comprehensive support programs for economic diversification and workforce retraining. Countries like Germany and Spain have established dedicated funds and institutions to manage this process, though outcomes have been mixed.</p><p><b>[G]</b> Corporate procurement of renewable energy has emerged as a major driver of the transition. Major technology companies including Google, Apple, and Amazon have committed to powering their operations with 100% renewable energy and are among the largest corporate buyers of wind and solar power through long-term purchase agreements. These commitments have ripple effects throughout supply chains, as companies increasingly require their suppliers to adopt renewable energy as well.</p><p><b>[H]</b> Despite remarkable progress, the pace of the energy transition remains insufficient to meet the goals of the Paris Agreement. The International Energy Agency projects that current policies will lead to global warming of approximately 2.4°C by 2100, significantly exceeding the 1.5°C target. Accelerating the transition will require not only continued technological innovation but also stronger policy frameworks, increased investment, and sustained political will across jurisdictions.</p>',
 '[{"label":"1","text":"Large technology corporations are using their purchasing power to drive demand for clean energy throughout their business networks."},{"label":"2","text":"The fact that sunshine and wind are not constant presents a fundamental difficulty for renewable energy systems."},{"label":"3","text":"Regions dependent on coal mining need support for economic restructuring as fossil fuel use declines."},{"label":"4","text":"The current speed of change is not enough to limit global temperature rise to the target set in the Paris Agreement."},{"label":"5","text":"Solar panel costs have dropped by about ninety percent since 2010 due to manufacturing scale and technical advances."},{"label":"6","text":"Countries that currently rely on petroleum exports face profound threats to their economic models."},{"label":"7","text":"The largest wind turbines now reach heights exceeding 250 meters and can supply electricity to thousands of households."},{"label":"8","text":"The most commonly used method of storing large amounts of electricity involves pumping water to higher elevations."},{"label":"9","text":"The shift to clean energy is one of the biggest economic changes since industrialization began."},{"label":"10","text":"Key minerals needed for batteries and clean energy technology create new forms of international dependence."}]',
 'G,D,F,H,B,E,C,D,A,E',
 '1→G(corporate procurement) 2→D(intermittency) 3→F(just transition coal) 4→H(Paris Agreement 2.4°C) 5→B(solar 90% reduction) 6→E(fossil fuel exporters) 7→C(250m wind turbines) 8→D(pumped hydro) 9→A(Industrial Revolution comparison) 10→E(lithium cobalt supply chains)',
 7, '长篇匹配,可再生能源,气候变化,经济');

