-- 仔细阅读 Passage 2: 社交媒体与注意力
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>The Attention Economy</h4><p>The contemporary digital landscape operates according to the logic of the attention economy, in which the most valuable resource is not information—which is abundant and essentially free—but human attention, which is finite and increasingly scarce. Technology companies compete intensely to capture and monetize this attention, deploying sophisticated algorithms designed to maximize user engagement rather than user welfare.</p><p>The mechanisms by which digital platforms capture attention are well-documented. Variable reward schedules, similar to those used in slot machines, keep users checking their devices compulsively. Infinite scroll features eliminate natural stopping points. Autoplay functions reduce the friction of continued engagement. Notification systems exploit social reciprocity norms, creating a sense of obligation to respond immediately. These design features are not accidental but are the products of extensive research and testing aimed at maximizing the time users spend on platforms.</p><p>The consequences of this attention capture extend far beyond wasted time. Research has documented significant negative effects on mental health, particularly among adolescents. The displacement of face-to-face social interaction, sleep, and physical activity by screen time has measurable impacts on psychological well-being. The fragmentation of attention impairs the capacity for deep, focused thinking—the kind of cognitive work essential for creativity, problem-solving, and learning.</p><p>Regulatory responses to the attention economy have been slow to develop. While some jurisdictions have introduced legislation addressing specific harms, such as cyberbullying and data privacy violations, few have directly confronted the underlying business model that incentivizes attention capture. Proposals for "attention rights" or "cognitive liberty" have begun to emerge in policy discussions, but they remain largely theoretical.</p><p>Individual strategies for reclaiming attention include digital minimalism—the intentional reduction of digital device usage—and the cultivation of practices that strengthen sustained attention, such as reading long-form content and engaging in activities that require extended concentration. However, placing the burden of resistance solely on individuals ignores the structural power of the platforms that have been engineered to be addictive. Meaningful change likely requires both individual agency and collective action to reshape the digital environment.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '仔细阅读文章：注意力经济', 7, '仔细阅读,社交媒体,心理健康'),

('CAREFUL_READING', 'According to the passage, what is the most valuable resource in the current digital economy?',
