 'C', '第四段指出虽然已有针对cyberbullying和data privacy的立法，但"few have directly confronted the underlying business model that incentivizes attention capture."', 7, '仔细阅读,主旨判断'),

-- 仔细阅读 Passage 3: 人工智能伦理
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>The Ethics of Artificial Intelligence</h4><p>As artificial intelligence systems become increasingly integrated into high-stakes decision-making—from medical diagnosis and criminal justice to hiring and loan approvals—the ethical dimensions of these technologies demand urgent attention. AI systems are not neutral tools; they reflect the values, assumptions, and biases embedded in their training data and design choices.</p><p>Algorithmic bias has emerged as a particularly troubling concern. When AI systems are trained on historical data that reflects existing social inequalities, they tend to reproduce and even amplify those inequalities. Facial recognition systems, for example, have been shown to have significantly higher error rates for people with darker skin tones, a disparity that can have grave consequences when such systems are used by law enforcement. Similarly, hiring algorithms trained on data from companies with predominantly male workforces have been found to systematically disadvantage female applicants.</p><p>The problem of explainability—often called the "black box" problem—presents another fundamental challenge. Many of the most powerful AI systems, particularly deep learning models, make decisions through processes that are opaque even to their creators. This lack of transparency becomes deeply problematic when AI systems make consequential decisions affecting individuals rights and opportunities. If a loan application is rejected or a prison sentence is lengthened based on an AI recommendation, the affected individual has a right to understand the reasoning behind that decision.</p><p>Various frameworks for ethical AI have been proposed. The European Union AI Act, the first comprehensive AI regulation by a major jurisdiction, adopts a risk-based approach, imposing stricter requirements on AI applications deemed to pose higher risks to fundamental rights. Principles of fairness, accountability, and transparency have been widely endorsed by industry, academia, and civil society. However, translating these high-level principles into concrete technical standards and enforceable regulations remains a work in progress.</p><p>The question of responsibility is particularly complex in AI systems. When an autonomous vehicle causes an accident, or when an AI medical diagnostic system makes a fatal error, who bears responsibility? The engineer who designed the algorithm? The company that deployed it? The institution that relied on its recommendation? Traditional legal frameworks based on individual human agency and intent are ill-equipped to address these questions, necessitating new legal and regulatory approaches.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '仔细阅读文章：AI伦理', 8, '仔细阅读,AI,伦理,法律'),

('CAREFUL_READING', 'According to the passage, AI systems are described as "not neutral tools" because:',
