-- 仔细阅读 Passage 4: 行为经济学
('CAREFUL_READING', '<h4>Behavioral Economics and Public Policy</h4><p>Traditional economic theory rests on the assumption that humans are rational actors who make decisions by carefully weighing costs and benefits to maximize their personal utility. Behavioral economics, which integrates insights from psychology into economic analysis, has systematically challenged this assumption, demonstrating that human decision-making is subject to systematic biases, cognitive limitations, and emotional influences.</p><p>One of the most influential concepts in behavioral economics is the "nudge," popularized by Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein in their 2008 book of the same name. A nudge is a subtle change in the choice architecture—the environment in which decisions are made—that steers people toward beneficial behaviors without restricting their freedom of choice. Classic examples include placing healthier foods at eye level in cafeterias to encourage better dietary choices, or automatically enrolling employees in retirement savings plans while allowing them to opt out.</p><p>The application of behavioral insights to public policy has spread rapidly across the globe. The United Kingdom established the Behavioural Insights Team (often called the "Nudge Unit") in 2010, and similar units have been created in governments from Australia to Singapore to the United States. These units have applied behavioral principles to increase tax compliance, encourage organ donation, reduce energy consumption, and improve public health outcomes.</p><p>However, the nudge approach has attracted significant criticism. Some critics argue that nudges, while preserving formal freedom of choice, represent a form of manipulation that undermines individual autonomy. If people are influenced to make choices without being fully aware of the influences upon them, can those choices truly be considered free? Others worry about the potential for nudges to be used for purposes that benefit private interests rather than public welfare—a concern sometimes labeled "sludge" when choice architecture is used to exploit rather than help people.</p><p>Proponents respond that choice architecture is inevitable: every presentation of options embodies some design choice that will influence decisions, whether intentionally or not. The absence of deliberate choice architecture is itself a choice, and often a worse one. The relevant question, they argue, is not whether to influence choices but how to do so transparently and in ways that serve legitimate public purposes. The debate ultimately reflects deeper philosophical questions about the nature of freedom, the role of government, and what it means to make a genuinely autonomous decision.</p>',
 NULL, 'PASSAGE', '仔细阅读文章：行为经济学与助推理论', 8, '仔细阅读,经济学,心理学,政策'),

